#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147333 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "a3030491-423d-5633-8208-b373bfee3b06" :question/math-academy-id "e-3892" :question/problem "At a shopping mall, the rate at which people are entering the mall is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 300t^2+50,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per hour, and $t>0$ is the number of hours since the mall opened. In addition, people are leaving the mall at a steady rate of $25$ people per hour. \n\nI. Calculate the number of people that entered the mall in the first two hours.\n\nII. Calculate the net change in the number of people inside the mall in the first 2 hours." :question/worked-solution "**Part 1**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50.$ To find out how many people entered the mall between $t=0$ and $t=2,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}300{t}^{2}+50\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={100{t}^{3}+50t|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(100\\cdot {2}^{3}+50(2))-(0) \\\\  & =(800+100) \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Part 2**\n\nPeople are entering the mall at a rate of $r(t) = 300t^2+50$ people per hour, and are leaving the mall at a rate of $25$ people per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of people inside the mall, which we'll call $R(t),$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **net change** in the number of people, we integrate $R(t)$ between $t=0$ and $t=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}R(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)-25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(t)\\,\\text{d}t}_{⏟}}_{900}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =900-[25t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =900-50 \\\\  & =850\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, over the first two hours, there was a net increase of $850$ people."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147334 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "d007e9ad-3fd3-5ae2-864a-ef89ea01785c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3893" :question/problem "The rate at which guests are arriving at a restaurant one evening is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t) = 6t^2+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since the restaurant opened. Guests are also leaving at a steady rate of $6$ guests per hour. If there were $50$ guests in the restaurant when it opened, how many guests will be in the restaurant after $1$ hour?" :question/worked-solution "Guests are entering the restaurant at a rate of $6t^2+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $6$ guests per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}+2-6 \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of guests after $1$ hour, we substitute $t=1$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =50+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50+{[2{x}^{3}-4x]|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =50+[(2(1)^{3}-4(1))-(0)] \\\\  & =50-2 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147335 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/id #uuid "74125c40-ecdd-5453-9c49-13567fb4621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3894" :question/problem "In example 2, we saw that the number of guests in a restaurant is given by\n\n$$\nT(t) = 50 + \\int_0^t \\left(6x^2-4\\right)\\,\\textrm d x\\,.\n$$\n\nGiven that $T(t)$ has a local minimum for some $t>0,$ find the time when the number of guests is at its minimum value." :question/worked-solution "To find the time when the number of guests is minimized, we need to solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $T(t)$ with respect to $t$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(50)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{x}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2-4 = 0\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad t = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}\n$$\n\nSo we have $2$ critical points $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac 2 3}$ and $t=0$ (the endpoint). But since the local minimum exists for $t>0,$ $t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}}$ must be the minimum value."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "265ecd0f-3972-5067-ba96-bdb3ba407533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$84\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477810} {:answer/id #uuid "6af48d20-3f33-58ec-bec7-1869e9128152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$72\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477955} {:answer/id #uuid "705d379f-401c-56b5-83bb-77100e1e7d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$46\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477969} {:answer/id #uuid "b618c6e7-ba4f-525e-8f18-44e3693235b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$64\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478093} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ef92e4-bb3a-5c96-890c-63549a292b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$52\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "705d379f-401c-56b5-83bb-77100e1e7d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$46\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477969} :answer-field/id #uuid "a852c03b-c366-522d-a206-00a4bf8a6536" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6686da9c-8c33-5aec-9383-87dd4a554b8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140541" :question/problem "The rate $r(t)$ at which the balance in a particular savings account grows is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=20{e}^{0.05t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in thousands of dollars per year, and $t>0$ is the time in years.\n\nTo the nearest thousand dollars, how much did the account's balance increase between years $t=2$ and $t=4?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** of the account's balance $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the balance between the times $t=2$ and $t=4,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}20{e}^{0.05t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={20\\,\\frac{{e}^{0.05t}}{0.05}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400{e}^{0.05t}{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =400({e}^{0.2}-{e}^{0.1}) \\\\  & ≈46\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, between years $t=2$ and $t=4,$ the account's balance increased by $$46\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d162f3e-17aa-5cff-8131-03e0538a85c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ thousand people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477788} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5f41bb-197a-564d-9072-fe7cb4a595c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ thousand people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477890} {:answer/id #uuid "6b1654eb-06a9-5f87-a22a-78022a308773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ thousand people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477956} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3fcb94-41a8-56cf-b430-5d4d13ebc53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ thousand people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478073} {:answer/id #uuid "f5caddf5-f3b5-5c92-b7bc-485bbd3ccf7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$24$ thousand people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae3fcb94-41a8-56cf-b430-5d4d13ebc53c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ thousand people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478073} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d756dbc-7220-5954-bceb-059c17f94d5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b00e0f7a-d41e-5bb8-bfda-7014077ce7a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-140545" :question/problem "The rate at which the number of visitors at a particular festival changes is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=4t+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in thousands of people per hour, and $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the festival opened its gates.\n\nHow many thousands of people did the number of visitors increase by between hours $t=1$ and $t=3?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given a **rate of change** in the number of visitors $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the number of visitors between $t=1$ and $t=3$ hours, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4t+2)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(2{t}^{2}+2t)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(2(3)^{2}+2(3))-(2(1)^{2}+2(1)) \\\\  & =18+6-2-2 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between hours $t=1$ and $t=3,$ the number of visitors increased by $20$ thousand people."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "acf3dade-2a70-5646-9aeb-7e0d84d7da28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acf3dade-2a70-5646-9aeb-7e0d84d7da28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478069} :answer-field/id #uuid "7660da0d-9c61-5542-8994-8a70e34620e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "297141b4-8227-5679-ae9f-447866ab2753" :question/math-academy-id "q-346823" :question/problem "A homeowner is tracking the rate at which electricity is being used in her house. The rate $r(t)$ at which electricity is consumed is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2{t}^{2}-8t+10,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in kilowatts, and $0<t<6$ is the time in hours since $9\\,\\text{am}.$\n\nBetween $t=0$ and $t=3$ hours,\n{{field-1}} kilowatt-hours were consumed." :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** of electricity consumption $r(t).$ To find the **net amount** of electricity consumed by the house between the times $t=0$ and $t=3,$ we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{t}^{2}-8t+10)\\,\\text{d}t & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{8}{2}{t}^{2}+10t)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}+10t)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3}-4(3)^{2}+10(3))-(0-0+0) \\\\  & =18-36+30 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=0$ and $t=3$ hours, $12$ kilowatt-hours were consumed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07559793-050c-5b0e-8132-30f4862a98c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^2-10t+500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07559793-050c-5b0e-8132-30f4862a98c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^2-10t+500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477749} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ad5fb33-14ac-5b7e-b4c8-54413afbb058" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d1f89285-0002-5f93-8ef8-535c491c51d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-346832" :question/problem "Environmental scientists are studying the population of fish in a lake.\nThey observe that the fish population increases due to reproduction at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=40{t}^{2}+6t,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in fish per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the study began.\nAt the same time, $10$ fish are lost each month due to predation.\n\nIf there were initially $500$ fish in the lake, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of fish in the lake $t$ months after the beginning of the study.\n\n$P(t)=\\frac{40}{3}{t}^{3}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The population of the lake is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ fish per month and is decreasing at a rate of $10$ fish per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of fish is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-10 \\\\  & =40{t}^{2}+6t-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of fish after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =500+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(40{x}^{2}+6x-10)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =500+{\\int }_{0}^{t}40{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{t}6x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =500+{40\\,\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{t}+{6\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{t}-10x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =500+\\frac{40}{3}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-10t \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-10t+500\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac85ce10-f4c9-5672-90a4-b9622430ecb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac85ce10-f4c9-5672-90a4-b9622430ecb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478066} :answer-field/id #uuid "6189fd16-3c38-5ad8-9fdf-a852344c52b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdea6428-2f62-5007-88f6-e327c0da6446" :question/math-academy-id "q-349763" :question/problem "A factory is tracking the rate at which plastic pellets are produced. The rate $r(t)$ at which pellets are produced is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=0.4\\sqrt{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in kilograms per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes.\n\nBetween $t=1$ and $t=10$ minutes, to one decimal place,\n{{field-1}} kilograms were produced." :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** in the amount of plastic pellets produced, $r(t).$ To find the amount of plastic pellets produced between the times $t=1$ and $t=10$ minutes, we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}0.4\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={0.4\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & ={\\frac{0.8}{3}{t}^{3/2}\\,|}_{1}^{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.8}{3}(10)^{3/2}-\\frac{0.8}{3}(1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{10}}{3}-\\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & ≈8.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place. \n\nTherefore, between $t=1$ and $t=10$ minutes, $8.2$ kilograms were produced."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c08c9983-b9b2-5f98-8bea-9e4ff5676410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "149" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c08c9983-b9b2-5f98-8bea-9e4ff5676410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "149" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478110} :answer-field/id #uuid "357b00fc-4fd9-500a-8159-52e1695534f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70deaba5-9010-5ebe-8348-672c8fc97ab0" :question/math-academy-id "q-349971" :question/problem "The rate at which customers enter a store one afternoon is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=6{e}^{t}+3,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in customers per hour and $t>0$ is the time in hours since $1\\,\\text{p.m}.$\nCustomers are also leaving the store at a steady rate of $5$ customers per hour.\n\nIf there were $40$ customers in the store at $1\\,\\text{p.m},$ how many customers will be there at $4\\,\\text{p.m}?$\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, there will be \n{{field-1}}  customers in the store at $4\\,\\text{p.m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Customers are entering the store at a rate of $6{e}^{t}+3$ customers per hour and are leaving at a rate of $5$ customers per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of customers (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-5 \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}+3-5 \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of customers** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(6{e}^{t}-2)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of packages after $3$ hours ($4\\,\\text{p.m}$), we substitute $t=3$ into the above and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6{e}^{t}-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =40+(6{e}^{t}-2t){|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =40+[(6{e}^{3}-2(3))-(6{e}^{0}-2(0))] \\\\  & =40+6{e}^{3}-12 \\\\  & =6{e}^{3}+28 \\\\  & ≈149\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, there will be $149$ customers in the store at $4\\,\\text{p.m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcac5d0e-f796-5fa5-9733-a3ca8c3b5eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "260-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcac5d0e-f796-5fa5-9733-a3ca8c3b5eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "260-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318305} :answer-field/id #uuid "17091112-eedf-50be-b897-838934035785" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "894497b2-7451-5370-bf78-0958e2308d18" :question/math-academy-id "q-349983" :question/problem "A zoo is tracking the number of penguins in a habitat.\nThey observe that new penguins arrive at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=30{e}^{-t/2},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in penguins per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the tracking began.\nAt the same time, $3$ penguins are transferred out each month.\n\nIf there were initially $200$ penguins in the habitat, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of penguins in the habitat $t$ months after the tracking began.\n\n$P(t)=-60{e}^{-t/2}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The number of penguins is increasing at a rate of $r(t)$ penguins per month and is decreasing at a rate of $3$ penguins per month. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the number of penguins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-3 \\\\  & =30{e}^{-t/2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total number $P(t)$ of penguins after $t$ months, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(30{e}^{-x/2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration.\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}30{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+(-60{e}^{-x/2}){|}_{0}^{t}-3x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =200+(60-60{e}^{-t/2})-3t \\\\  & =-60{e}^{-t/2}+260-3t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a8c25b5-abd4-5d08-8077-b963def9cc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a8c25b5-abd4-5d08-8077-b963def9cc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478016} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6aa069d-3562-534f-8fc8-0c90efa66f3b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c605502-884c-5e1d-93dc-00f41bf6b0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c605502-884c-5e1d-93dc-00f41bf6b0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "73" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477891} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7203782-f2cc-5e71-8798-2bfaadb03f35" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c581124e-b7b1-5b7e-bfb5-1ff3fee7303f" :question/math-academy-id "q-349995" :question/problem "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?\n\nThe maximum number of people is\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nhours." :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(8-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n3(8-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =25+3{\\int }_{0}^{4}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25+3{[8x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =25+3[(8\\cdot 4-{4}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =25+3\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =73\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $25<73$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $73$, and this will happen after $4$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dedb264-eb4b-554b-b1fc-c33940aacd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dedb264-eb4b-554b-b1fc-c33940aacd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317884} :answer-field/id #uuid "014eb3f4-5b0d-5bdd-b80d-f837749852b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf14b68b-61e2-566a-924c-53e4c88d9ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf14b68b-61e2-566a-924c-53e4c88d9ee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318250} :answer-field/id #uuid "6858d1c2-03bc-54aa-aad0-5ca01fbf3806" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "799a9b42-4f6e-5c6d-8236-7829c2135c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-350001" :question/problem "The value $P(t)$ of a painting is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is measured in dollars, and $t>0$ is the time (in years) since the painting was created. \n\nAt what time is the value a minimum, and how much does the painting cost at this time, rounded to the nearest dollar?\n\nThe minimum value is\n$$$\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nyears." :question/worked-solution "To find when the value is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={3}^{t/4}-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{t/4}-27 & =0 \\\\ {3}^{t/4} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{t}{4} & =3 \\\\ t & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=12.$ To find out the value of the painting when $t=12,$ we figure out $P(12):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{12}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250+{[\\frac{4\\cdot {3}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-27x]|}_{0}^{12} \\\\  & =250+[(\\frac{4\\cdot 27}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-324)-(\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})] \\\\  & =250-324+\\frac{108-4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-74+\\frac{104}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSince $21<250,$ we conclude that the minimum value is $$\\,21$, and this happens after $12$ years."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "523dd753-96ea-5ba3-8d46-25c691f1a43f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{25}\\frac{2}{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477904} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea4c6b4-7072-5186-ab7e-62d147395265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{25}(25+\\frac{2}{1+3x})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477964} {:answer/id #uuid "820ca443-32ae-5cbe-9690-f2c16e2fc485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{2}{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478005} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e393a4-5848-57a3-8d0b-cd06a591a84b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{t}(25+\\frac{2}{1+3x})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478194} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3c909f-4352-5ed5-a906-92f7cfe73109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{2}{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "820ca443-32ae-5cbe-9690-f2c16e2fc485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{2}{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478005} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a636dfd-5828-555f-99ef-f4c865bb1960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3cbd327e-e7fe-5c08-8abb-efce49ecca7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-62813" :question/problem "The price of crude oil, measured in dollars per barrel, increases at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{2}{1+3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the start of a particular year.\n\nIf the price was $$25$ per barrel at the start of the year, which expression gives the price of crude oil $t$ months since the start of the year?" :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** of the oil price $r(t).$ To find the **total price** after $t$ months (call it $P(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{2}{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{2}{1+3x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3701e1d6-1e68-56c0-9d1f-e84f7df9cff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$460$ PSI" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477839} {:answer/id #uuid "3b250913-3582-58e5-acd1-e7a34d408504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$110$ PSI" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477844} {:answer/id #uuid "89ae45f6-911e-52bd-8125-c2c5e66244ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$450$ PSI" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478012} {:answer/id #uuid "97696b88-83aa-55cf-ac2b-65f8ecc3154c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$470$ PSI" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478030} {:answer/id #uuid "f04701d9-5b5f-5a52-8270-d0538891b850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$100$ PSI" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97696b88-83aa-55cf-ac2b-65f8ecc3154c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$470$ PSI" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478030} :answer-field/id #uuid "737c0260-d4e4-5d9a-816e-4ee7faf17353" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08b5b55f-08a9-5a88-9bb3-7419f5e97ed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-62823" :question/problem "Air is being pumped into a rubber speed boat. The rate at which the pressure increases in the boat is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=2t+4,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in PSI per second and $t>0$ is the time in seconds since the pumping started.\n\nThe boat has a slow puncture which releases air at a rate of $1$ PSI every second.\n\nIf the boat had a pressure of $10$ PSI in the beginning, how much pressure does it have after $20$ seconds?" :question/worked-solution "The pressure increases at a rate of $2t+4$ PSI per second and is decreasing at a rate of $1$ PSI per second. Therefore, the **rate of change of pressure** (call it $R(t)$) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-1 \\\\  & =2t+4-1 \\\\  & =2t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total pressure** after $t$ seconds (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the quantity of pressure after $20$ seconds, we substitute $t=20$ into the above and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(20) & =10+{\\int }_{0}^{20}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+{[{x}^{2}+3x]|}_{0}^{20} \\\\  & =10+[({20}^{2}+3(20))] \\\\  & =10+460 \\\\  & =470\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d3c131e-de0e-5949-b6c8-2903327de43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,100$ people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478039} {:answer/id #uuid "9ef6b46b-f4e0-58a5-a73a-4927bc969543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,550$ people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478043} {:answer/id #uuid "b49a5738-be51-56ce-b6a2-db97d2581c07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,005$ people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478088} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a45ad1-33a0-5e92-b742-82417febf4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,800$ people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478111} {:answer/id #uuid "cc117233-daac-5427-b7ef-ccd608c5d85e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$205$ people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ef6b46b-f4e0-58a5-a73a-4927bc969543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,550$ people" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478043} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dbcb7c1-967b-5d0d-a8d8-bb377d84906e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "722ff44e-d1e5-509c-bcea-de9f4cf913cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-90730" :question/problem "People enter the ballpark at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function \n\n$$\n10t+5,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in people per minute,  and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the ballpark gates were opened to the public.  \n\nHow many people entered the ballpark between $t=10$ minutes and $t=20$  minutes." :question/worked-solution "We're given the **rate of change** of the number of people $r(t).$ To find the **net change** in the number of people who entered the ballpark between  $t=10$ minutes and $t=20$ minutes,  we integrate $r(t)$ between these two limits. This gives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{10}^{20}10x+5\\,\\text{d}t & =5({x}^{2}+x){|}_{10}^{20} \\\\  & =5[({20}^{2}+20)-({10}^{2}+10)] \\\\  & =5\\cdot (420-110) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (310) \\\\  & =1\\,550\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, between $t=10$ minute and $t=20$ minutes,  a total of $1\\,550$ people entered the ballpark."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c1be2e7-4656-5b08-8728-4c2446b63172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The maximium is $48$ people  after $4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477785} {:answer/id #uuid "4c45c107-089b-5db6-a331-3f2182bae6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The maximium is $73$ people  after $4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477889} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ff893f-65d4-5361-8ae9-e6288c82959f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The maximium is $55$ people  after $3$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478090} {:answer/id #uuid "c9c48e52-e51f-5425-8d59-9ff2593bbf84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The maximium is $75$ people  after $3$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478134} {:answer/id #uuid "f541ed1a-b24f-5a07-b763-c68082f76bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The maximium is $65$ people  after $5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c45c107-089b-5db6-a331-3f2182bae6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The maximium is $73$ people  after $4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477889} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b38b8cf-d4c5-5e5c-902d-fd247fcc4704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "480dd21a-6ad7-5569-99ae-370c6e08eaac" :question/math-academy-id "q-90749" :question/problem "On a certain afternoon, the total number of people $N(t)$ visiting the city's zoo is given by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since noon.  \n\nAt what time is the number of people in the zoo at a maximum, and how many people are at the zoo at that time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people at the zoo is at maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25+3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(25)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(8-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n3(8-2t)=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many people are in the zoo when $t=4,$ we figure out $N(4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(4) & =25+3{\\int }_{0}^{4}(8-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25+3{[8x-{x}^{2}]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =25+3[(8\\cdot 4-{4}^{2})-(0)] \\\\  & =25+3\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =73\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $25<73$, we conclude that the maximum number of people at the zoo is $73$, and this will happen after $4$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "469565fb-6d27-567b-b115-17fe2967823a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $230$ fish after $5$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355288} {:answer/id #uuid "90fa05bc-36a8-547f-aaf2-47eabb226e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $230$ fish after $1$ week" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355945} {:answer/id #uuid "a058f107-94e7-532a-8e45-31294473dddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $214$ fish after $3$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356086} {:answer/id #uuid "d342dcb4-5a95-5454-8d7c-b07c177b7c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $215$ fish after $4$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356509} {:answer/id #uuid "e59883a6-26da-5b2d-a2ad-67a91b6e4325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $218$ fish after $2$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a058f107-94e7-532a-8e45-31294473dddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $214$ fish after $3$ weeks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356086} :answer-field/id #uuid "c819c218-6e65-5a0e-be39-91f469445031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f28ae95-03b9-593b-a6e6-a24580ae5aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-90756" :question/problem "A man stocked a pond with $250$ fish. After $t$ weeks, the number of fish in the pond is given by the function \n\n$$\nF(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in weeks) since the man put the fish in the pond.\n\nAt what time is the number of fish in the pond at a minimum, and how many fish are there at that time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of fish in the pond is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8t-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n8t-24=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many fish are when $t=3,$ we figure out $F(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(3) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(8x-24)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250{[4{x}^{2}-24x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =250+[(4(3)^{2}-24(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =250-36 \\\\  & =214\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $214<250$, we conclude that the minimum number of fish in the pond is $214$, and this will happen after $3$ weeks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "797df782-68e4-5946-a5d3-719aacd38fd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $150$ people after $5$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355726} {:answer/id #uuid "8b39583c-7632-5314-aab0-b5c8f4813e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $200$ people after $2$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355896} {:answer/id #uuid "944fd6a9-bc5d-51fc-ae21-9b1361fccffd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $200$ people after $4$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355970} {:answer/id #uuid "9a1e95e1-91bb-5808-97fa-1c6f520895f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $100$ people after $3$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356033} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5a5011-dcd0-5823-8c28-0c3a166590aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $500$ people after $1$ hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a1e95e1-91bb-5808-97fa-1c6f520895f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The minimum is $100$ people after $3$ hours" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356033} :answer-field/id #uuid "715ea109-f7b4-5a47-ad9e-f36f04af9b6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0121e26f-4108-56d0-ac26-f4c71b23dab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-90758" :question/problem "The total number of people $V(t)$ who have visited a certain shopping center after $t$ hours is given by the function\n\n$$\nV(t)=1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time (in hours) since the shopping center opened its doors to the public.\n\nAt what time is the number of people visiting the shopping center at a minimum, and how many people are in the shopping center at that time?" :question/worked-solution "To find when the number of people is at minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1\\,000)+100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =100\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =100(2t-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n100(2t-6)=0\\,⟹\\,t=3\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=3.$ To find out how many people are in the shopping center when $t=3,$ we figure out $V(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(3) & =1\\,000+100{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+100{[{x}^{2}-6x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+100[({3}^{2}-6(3))-(0)] \\\\  & =1\\,000-900 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $100<1\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $100$, and this will happen after $3$ hours."}]}
